[{"type":"heading","data":{"textEn":"Tool Use and Function Calling — Designing Tool Interfaces Models Can Use","textKn":"Tool Use and Function Calling — Designing Tool Interfaces Models Can Use","level":"H1"}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Lesson Info","headingKn":"Lesson ಮಾಹಿತಿ","bodyEn":"• Type: Concept + Build · Language: Python (stdlib only) · Time: ~28 minutes · Part 2 of 3 in Tool Use and Function Calling","bodyKn":"• Type: Concept + Build · Language: Python (stdlib only) · Time: ~28 ನಿಮಿಷಗಳು · Part 2 of 3 in Tool Use and Function Calling","pillsEn":"Schema Validation,Coercion,Error Taxonomy,ToolResult","pillsKn":"Schema Validation,Coercion,Error Taxonomy,ToolResult"}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Validation, Coercion, Authorization and Sandboxing Are Four Different Things","headingKn":"Validation, Coercion, Authorization, Sandboxing — ನಾಲ್ಕೂ ಬೇರೆ ವಿಷಯಗಳು","bodyEn":"Validation checks shape (required fields, types, allowed values). Coercion narrowly converts compatible representations ("101" to 101) without guessing. Authorization checks whether this caller is allowed to invoke this tool with these arguments at all. Sandboxing limits what the tool execution itself can touch (filesystem, network, time). Conflating them is a common source of security bugs — a tool that validates perfectly can still be unauthorized or unsandboxed.","bodyKn":"Validation ಆಕಾರ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. Coercion ಹೊಂದಾಣಿಕೆ ಪ್ರತಿನಿಧಿತ್ವಗಳನ್ನೂ ಕಿರಿದಾಗಿ ಪರಿವರ್ತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. Authorization caller ಗೆ ಅನುಮತಿ ಇದೆಯೇ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. Sandboxing execution ಅನ್ನೂ ಮಿತಿಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಇವನ್ನು ಗೊಂದಲಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೇ ಸುರಕ್ಷತಾ bugs ya ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ ಮೂಲ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Coercion Must Be Narrow and Deterministic","headingKn":"Coercion ಕಿರಿದಾಗಿ ಮತ್ತು Deterministic ಆಗಿರಬೇಕು","bodyEn":"A good tool layer converts "101" to the integer 101 because that conversion is unambiguous, but refuses to guess that "banana" means any particular number. Narrow coercion accepts exactly the representations that have one obvious correct interpretation, and rejects everything else as a validation error rather than silently defaulting.","bodyKn":"ಒಳ್ಳೆ tool layer "101" ಅನ್ನೂ integer 101 ಗೆ ಪರಿವರ್ತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಏಕೆಂದರೆ ಅದೂ ಅಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಿಲ್ಲ, ಆದರೆ "banana" ಅನ್ನೂ ಯಾವುದೂ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ ಎಂದೂ ಊಹಿಸುವುದಿಲ್ಲ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"additionalProperties:false Rejects Unexpected Fields","headingKn":"additionalProperties:false ಅನಿರೀಕ್ಷಿತ Fields ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ","bodyEn":"Without this constraint, a model that hallucinates an extra argument (or a prompt-injected instruction that tries to smuggle one in) would silently pass through. Explicitly rejecting any field not named in the schema turns a silent risk into a loud, catchable validation error.","bodyKn":"ಇದೂ ಇಲ್ಲದಿದ್ದರೆ, ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ argument ಕಲ್ಪಿಸುವ model ಸುಮ್ಮನೆ ಹಾದುಹೋಗುತ್ತಿತ್ತು. Schema ನಲ್ಲಿ ಹೆಸರಿಸದ ಯಾವುದೂ field ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸುವುದೇ ಮೌನ ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ಗಟ್ಟಿಯಾದ, ಹಿಡಿಯಬಹುದಾದ ದೋಷವಾಗಿ ಬದಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"A Structured Error Taxonomy Turns Failures Into Observations","headingKn":"Structured ದೋಷ Taxonomy ವಿಫಲತೆಗಳನ್ನೂ Observations ಆಗಿ ಬದಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ","bodyEn":"Tagging every failure with a kind — unknown_tool, validation_error, execution_error, or timeout — instead of letting an exception crash the loop lets the result flow back to the model as a normal observation it can read and often self-correct from, exactly like the agent-loop pattern from earlier modules.","bodyKn":"ಪ್ರತಿ ವಿಫಲತೆಯನ್ನೂ kind ಜೊತೆ ಟ್ಯಾಗ್ ಮಾಡುವುದು — unknown_tool, validation_error, execution_error, timeout — exception loop ಕ್ರಾಶ್ ಮಾಡುವ ಬದಲು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಮಾದರಿಗೆ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ observation ಆಗಿ ಹಿಂತಿರುಗಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ."}},{"type":"table","data":{"headingEn":"Tool Error Taxonomy","rows":"Kind|When it fires
unknown_tool|The model named a tool that does not exist in the registry
validation_error|Arguments fail schema checks: missing field, bad type, unexpected field, bad enum
execution_error|The tool ran but raised an exception during real execution
timeout|The tool call exceeded its allotted time budget"}},{"type":"code","data":{"filename":"tool_validation.py","headingEn":"A Small JSON-Schema-Style Validator: Coercion Success and Rejection","headingKn":"ಸಣ್ಣ JSON-Schema-Style Validator","descEn":"A minimal validator enforces required fields, narrow integer coercion, enum checks, and additionalProperties:false, run against a coercion-success case, a coercion-rejection case, and an unexpected-field rejection.","descKn":"ಕನಿಷ್ಠ validator ಅಗತ್ಯ fields, ಕಿರಿದಾದ integer coercion, enum ಪರಿಶೀಲನೆ, ಮತ್ತು additionalProperties:false ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಮೂರೂ ಪ್ರಕರಣಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ.","code":"# Tool Use L2: schema validation, narrow coercion, enum, additionalProperties, error taxonomy
from dataclasses import dataclass, field

class ToolError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, kind, message):
        self.kind = kind  # unknown_tool | validation_error | execution_error | timeout
        self.message = message
        super().__init__(f"{kind}: {message}")

SCHEMA = {
    "type": "object",
    "required": ["city", "days"],
    "properties": {
        "city": {"type": "string"},
        "days": {"type": "integer"},
        "unit": {"type": "string", "enum": ["celsius", "fahrenheit"]},
    },
    "additionalProperties": False,
}

def coerce(value, expected_type):
    # narrow, deterministic coercion only -- no guessing
    if expected_type == "integer":
        if isinstance(value, bool):
            raise ToolError("validation_error", "boolean is not a valid integer")
        if isinstance(value, int):
            return value
        if isinstance(value, str) and value.strip().lstrip("-").isdigit():
            return int(value)
        raise ToolError("validation_error", f"cannot coerce {value!r} to integer")
    if expected_type == "string":
        if isinstance(value, str):
            return value
        raise ToolError("validation_error", f"expected string, got {type(value).__name__}")
    return value

def validate(args, schema):
    for field_name in schema["required"]:
        if field_name not in args:
            raise ToolError("validation_error", f"missing required field: {field_name}")
    if schema.get("additionalProperties") is False:
        extra = set(args) - set(schema["properties"])
        if extra:
            raise ToolError("validation_error", f"unexpected field(s): {sorted(extra)}")
    out = {}
    for key, value in args.items():
        prop = schema["properties"][key]
        coerced = coerce(value, prop["type"])
        if "enum" in prop and coerced not in prop["enum"]:
            raise ToolError("validation_error", f"{key}={coerced!r} not in allowed values {prop['enum']}")
        out[key] = coerced
    return out

# Case 1: genuine coercion success ("101" -> 101)
good_args = {"city": "Austin", "days": "101", "unit": "celsius"}
validated = validate(good_args, SCHEMA)
print("CASE 1 (coercion success):", validated)

# Case 2: genuine validation rejection ("banana" cannot become an int)
bad_args = {"city": "Austin", "days": "banana"}
try:
    validate(bad_args, SCHEMA)
except ToolError as e:
    print(f"CASE 2 (validation rejection): kind={e.kind} message={e.message!r}")

# Case 3: unexpected field rejected by additionalProperties:false
extra_args = {"city": "Austin", "days": "3", "zipcode": "78701"}
try:
    validate(extra_args, SCHEMA)
except ToolError as e:
    print(f"CASE 3 (additionalProperties rejection): kind={e.kind} message={e.message!r}")
"}},{"type":"output","data":{"output":"CASE 1 (coercion success): {'city': 'Austin', 'days': 101, 'unit': 'celsius'}
CASE 2 (validation rejection): kind=validation_error message="cannot coerce 'banana' to integer"
CASE 3 (additionalProperties rejection): kind=validation_error message="unexpected field(s): ['zipcode']""}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Each Case Genuinely Exercised a Different Rule","headingKn":"ಪ್ರತಿ ಪ್ರಕರಣ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬೇರೆ ನಿಯಮ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿತು","bodyEn":"Case 1 confirms the string "101" was deterministically coerced to the integer 101 — no guessing involved. Case 2 confirms "banana" was correctly rejected rather than silently coerced to some default. Case 3 confirms a field not in the schema ("zipcode") was caught by additionalProperties:false exactly as designed, with the specific offending field named in the error.","bodyKn":"Case 1 "101" ಅನ್ನೂ integer 101 ಗೆ deterministically coerce ಆಗಿದೆ ಎಂದೂ ದೃಢಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. Case 2 "banana" ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿತು ಎಂದೂ ದೃಢಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. Case 3 "zipcode" additionalProperties:false ಮೂಲಕ ಹಿಡಿಯಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿತು ಎಂದೂ ದೃಢಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ."}},{"type":"quiz","data":{"questions":[{"q":"In the executed code, what did "101" (a string) coerce to?","qKn":"ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿದ code ನಲ್ಲಿ "101" (string) ಏನೂ coerce ಆಯಿತು?","opts":["The string stayed "101"","The integer 101",""banana"","None, it was rejected"],"optsKn":["String "101" ಹಾಗೆ ಉಳಿಯಿತು","Integer 101",""banana"","ಯಾವುದೂ ಇಲ್ಲ, ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿತು"],"correct":1},{"q":"Why was "banana" rejected instead of coerced to some default integer?","qKn":""banana" ಏಕೆ ಯಾವುದೂ default integer ಗೆ coerce ಆಗದೆ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿತು?","opts":["Python cannot represent strings","Narrow coercion only accepts unambiguous representations, and "banana" has no obvious numeric meaning","It was a typo in the schema","All strings are rejected"],"optsKn":["ಪೈಥಾನ್ strings ಪ್ರತಿನಿಧಿಸಲಾಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ","ಕಿರಿದಾದ coercion ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರತಿನಿಧಿತ್ವಗಳ ಮಾತ್ರ ಸ್ವೀಕರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ","Schema ನಲ್ಲಿ typo","ಎಲ್ಲಾ strings ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಲ್ಪಡುತ್ತವೆ"],"correct":1},{"q":"What rule caught the "zipcode" field in Case 3?","qKn":"Case 3 ನಲ್ಲಿ "zipcode" field ಅನ್ನೂ ಯಾವ ನಿಯಮ ಹಿಡಿಯಿತು?","opts":["Enum validation","additionalProperties:false","Timeout","Authorization check"],"optsKn":["Enum validation","additionalProperties:false","Timeout","Authorization check"],"correct":1},{"q":"Why are validation, coercion, authorization, and sandboxing treated as distinct concerns?","qKn":"Validation, coercion, authorization, sandboxing ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಕಾಳಜಿಗಳೆಂದೂ ಪರಿಗಣಿಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ?","opts":["They are actually the same thing","A tool that passes validation can still be unauthorized or unsandboxed, so conflating them causes security bugs","Only coercion matters in practice","Sandboxing is never needed"],"optsKn":["ಅವೂ ಅಸಲಿಗೆ ಒಂದೇ","Validation ಪಾಸ್ ಆದ tool ಇನ್ನೂ unauthorized ಅಥವಾ unsandboxed ಆಗಿರಬಹುದು","Coercion ಮಾತ್ರ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಮುಖ್ಯ","Sandboxing ಎಂದಿಗೂ ಅಗತ್ಯ ಇಲ್ಲ"],"correct":1}]}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Key Takeaways","headingKn":"ಮುಖ್ಯ ಅಂಶಗಳು","bodyEn":"• Validation, coercion, authorization, and sandboxing are four distinct checks, not one.
• Coercion should be narrow and deterministic — convert only unambiguous representations.
• additionalProperties:false turns silently-passed extra fields into caught validation errors.
• A structured error taxonomy (unknown_tool/validation_error/execution_error/timeout) lets the model self-correct.","bodyKn":"• Validation, coercion, authorization, sandboxing ನಾಲ್ಕೂ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಪರಿಶೋಧನೆಗಳು.
• Coercion ಕಿರಿದಾಗಿ ಮತ್ತು deterministic ಆಗಿರಬೇಕು.
• additionalProperties:false ಮೌನ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ fields ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಡಿಯಲಾದ ದೋಷಗಳಾಗಿ ಬದಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.
• Structured ದೋಷ taxonomy model ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ವಯಂ-ಸರಿಪಡಿಸಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Authorization Happens Even When Validation Passes","headingKn":"Validation ಪಾಸ್ ಆದಾಗಲೂ Authorization ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ","bodyEn":"A tool call can have perfectly well-formed, schema-valid arguments and still be something the caller is not allowed to do — authorization is checked independently of, and typically after, validation, and a validation pass never implies an authorization pass.","bodyKn":"Tool ಕರೆ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಲಾದ, schema-valid arguments ಹೊಂದಬಹುದು ಮತ್ತು ಇನ್ನೂ caller ಗೆ ಅನುಮತಿ ಇಲ್ಲದ್ದಾಗಬಹುದು."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Role-Based Permissions Are the Simplest Authorization Model","headingKn":"Role-Based Permissions ಸರಳ Authorization ಮಾದರಿ","bodyEn":"Mapping each caller role to the specific set of tools it may invoke is the simplest workable authorization scheme — more sophisticated systems add per-resource ownership checks (can this user refund THIS specific order) on top, but the role check is the first, cheapest gate.","bodyKn":"ಪ್ರತಿ caller role ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೂ ಕರೆಯಬಹುದಾದ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ tools ಸೆಟ್‌ಗೆ ಜೋಡಿಸುವುದೇ ಸರಳ ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುವ authorization ಯೋಜನೆ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"The Error Kind "unauthorized" Should Not Leak Implementation Detail","headingKn":""unauthorized" ದೋಷ ಪ್ರಕಾರ Implementation ವಿವರ Leak ಮಾಡಬಾರದು","bodyEn":"An authorization rejection should tell the caller (or the model) that permission was denied, but need not explain the full permission model — unlike a validation error, where specificity helps the model self-correct, an authorization error is not something the model should be trying to work around by guessing a different role.","bodyKn":"Authorization ತಿರಸ್ಕರಣೆ caller ಗೆ ಅನುಮತಿ ನಿರಾಕರಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿತು ಎಂದೂ ಹೇಳಬೇಕು, ಆದರೆ ಪೂರ್ಣ ಅನುಮತಿ ಮಾದರಿ ವಿವರಿಸಬೇಕಾಗಿಲ್ಲ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Sandboxing Limits Blast Radius Even After Authorization Passes","headingKn":"Authorization ಪಾಸ್ ಆದ ನಂತರವೂ Sandboxing Blast Radius ಮಿತಿಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ","bodyEn":"Even a fully authorized, fully validated tool call should execute inside constraints (no arbitrary filesystem access, no unexpected network calls, resource/time limits) — sandboxing protects against bugs in the tool's own implementation, not just malicious or confused callers.","bodyKn":"ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ authorized, ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ validated tool ಕರೆ ಸಹ ಮಿತಿಗಳ ಒಳಗೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಬೇಕು — Sandboxing tool ya ಸ್ವಂತ implementation ನಲ್ಲಿ bugs ವಿರುದ್ಧ ರಕ್ಷಿಸುತ್ತದೆ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Audit Logging Every Authorization Decision","headingKn":"ಪ್ರತಿ Authorization ನಿರ್ಧಾರ Audit Logging","bodyEn":"Logging every authorization check (who, which tool, allowed or denied, when) separately from general application logs gives security review a clean trail to answer "who tried to do what" after an incident, without having to reconstruct it from general-purpose logs.","bodyKn":"ಪ್ರತಿ authorization ಪರಿಶೀಲನೆ ಲಾಗ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೇ ಸುರಕ್ಷತಾ ವಿಮರ್ಶೆಗೆ ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ ಜಾಡು ನೀಡುತ್ತದೆ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Least Privilege Applies to Agents Too","headingKn":"Least Privilege Agents ಗೆ ಸಹ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆ","bodyEn":"An agent acting on a user's behalf should be granted only the tools genuinely needed for its task, not the caller's full permission set by default — a research assistant agent should not inherit admin refund permissions just because the human operating it happens to have them.","bodyKn":"User ya ಪರವಾಗಿ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ agent ಗೆ ಅದರ ಕೆಲಸಕ್ಕೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬೇಕಾದ tools ಮಾತ್ರ ನೀಡಬೇಕು, caller ya ಪೂರ್ಣ permission set ಅಲ್ಲ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Revalidating Authorization on Every Call, Not Once Per Session","headingKn":"ಪ್ರತಿ ಕರೆಯ ಮೇಲೆ Authorization ಮರುಪರಿಶೀಲನೆ, ಪ್ರತಿ Session ಗೆ ಒಂದೂ ಬಾರಿ ಅಲ್ಲ","bodyEn":"Permissions can change mid-session (a role is revoked, an account is suspended); checking authorization once at session start and caching the result for the whole session risks acting on stale permissions — each tool call should re-check, even if that means a small repeated cost.","bodyKn":"Permissions session ya ಮಧ್ಯೆ ಬದಲಾಗಬಹುದು; session ಪ್ರಾರಂಭದಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದೂ ಬಾರಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ cache ಮಾಡುವುದೇ ಹಳೆಯ permissions ಮೇಲೆ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ ಅಪಾಯ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Prompt Injection Is an Authorization Problem in Disguise","headingKn":"Prompt Injection ವೇಷದಲ್ಲಿ Authorization ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ","bodyEn":"When untrusted content (a webpage, a document) tricks a model into calling a sensitive tool it should not, the real defense is not detecting the injected phrase in the text — it is ensuring the tool-call layer still enforces authorization and validation regardless of what the model was convinced to attempt.","bodyKn":"ಅವಿಶ್ವಾಸಾರ್ಹ content model ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೂ ಕರೆಯಬಾರದ tool ಕರೆಯಲು ಮೋಸಗೊಳಿಸಿದಾಗ, ನಿಜ ರಕ್ಷಣೆ tool-call ಪದರ ಇನ್ನೂ authorization ಮತ್ತು validation ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದೂ ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿಕೊಳ್ಳುವುದು."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"A Four-Layer Mental Model for Every Tool Call","headingKn":"ಪ್ರತಿ Tool ಕರೆಗೆ ನಾಲ್ಕೂ-ಪದರ ಮಾನಸಿಕ ಮಾದರಿ","bodyEn":"Every real tool call should pass, in order: (1) does this tool exist, (2) are the arguments well-formed (validation/coercion), (3) is this caller allowed to do this (authorization), (4) execute it under constraints (sandboxing) — skipping or merging these layers is where most tool-use security bugs originate.","bodyKn":"ಪ್ರತಿ ನಿಜ tool ಕರೆ ಕ್ರಮವಾಗಿ ಹಾದುಹೋಗಬೇಕು: (1) tool ಅಸ್ತಿತ್ವದಲ್ಲಿದೆಯೇ, (2) arguments ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆಯೇ, (3) caller ಗೆ ಅನುಮತಿ ಇದೆಯೇ, (4) ಮಿತಿಗಳ ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Testing Authorization With Both Positive and Negative Cases","headingKn":"Authorization Positive ಮತ್ತು Negative Cases ಎರಡೂ ಜೊತೆ ಪರೀಕ್ಷೆ","bodyEn":"A thorough test suite checks not just that an authorized role can call its allowed tools, but explicitly that an unauthorized role is genuinely rejected — a permission system with only positive tests can silently regress to allowing everything without any test failing.","bodyKn":"ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ test suite authorized role ಅದರ ಅನುಮತಿಸಿದ tools ಕರೆಯಬಹುದು ಎಂದೂ ಮಾತ್ರ ಅಲ್ಲ, unauthorized role ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಲ್ಪಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದೂ ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Different Tools Can Warrant Different Sandboxing Strength","headingKn":"ಬೇರೆ Tools ಬೇರೆ Sandboxing ಬಲ ಅರ್ಹಿಸಬಹುದು","bodyEn":"A read-only lookup tool against an internal, trusted database needs lighter sandboxing than a tool that executes arbitrary model-generated code or makes outbound network calls — matching sandbox strength to actual risk avoids both under-protecting dangerous tools and over-engineering harmless ones.","bodyKn":"ಆಂತರಿಕ, ವಿಶ್ವಾಸಾರ್ಹ database ವಿರುದ್ಧ read-only lookup tool ಅನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತ model-ಉತ್ಪಾದಿತ code ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವ tool ಗಿಂತ ಹಗುರ sandboxing ಬೇಕು."}},{"type":"concept","data":{"headingEn":"Coercion Rules Should Be Documented Alongside the Schema","headingKn":"Coercion ನಿಯಮಗಳ Schema ಜೊತೆ ದಾಖಲಿಸಬೇಕು","bodyEn":"Because narrow coercion rules (what exactly counts as convertible) are a design decision, not an obvious fact, documenting them next to each tool's schema prevents future maintainers from "fixing" a rejection by loosening coercion in a way that reintroduces ambiguous guessing.","bodyKn":"ಕಿರಿದಾದ coercion ನಿಯಮಗಳು ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ನಿರ್ಧಾರ, ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ ಸತ್ಯ ಅಲ್ಲ, ಆದ್ದರಿಂದ ಅವನ್ನೂ schema ಜೊತೆ ದಾಖಲಿಸುವುದೇ ಭವಿಷ್ಯದ maintainers ಗೆ ಸಹಾಯ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ."}},{"type":"table","data":{"headingEn":"Four Layers of a Safe Tool Call","rows":"Layer|Question it answers|Failure kind if it fails
Existence|Does this tool exist in the registry?|unknown_tool
Validation/coercion|Are arguments well-formed and unambiguous?|validation_error
Authorization|Is this caller allowed to do this?|unauthorized
Sandboxing|Does execution stay within safe limits?|execution_error / timeout"}},{"type":"table","data":{"headingEn":"Role-Based Tool Permissions (executed example)","rows":"Role|Allowed tools
support_agent|lookup_order
admin|lookup_order, issue_refund"}},{"type":"code","data":{"filename":"tool_authorization.py","headingEn":"Authorization Rejecting a Valid Call, Then Allowing the Same Call for a Different Role","headingKn":"Authorization ಒಂದೂ ಸರಿಯಾದ ಕರೆ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ನಂತರ ಬೇರೆ Role ಗೆ ಅದೇ ಕರೆ ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ","descEn":"The exact same well-formed issue_refund call is checked for two different roles: a support_agent is genuinely rejected despite perfectly valid arguments, while an admin with the same arguments is genuinely authorized and the tool executes.","descKn":"ಅದೇ ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಲಾದ issue_refund ಕರೆ ಎರಡೂ ಬೇರೆ roles ಗೆ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಲ್ಪಡುತ್ತದೆ: support_agent ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಲ್ಪಡುತ್ತದೆ, admin ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅನುಮತಿಸಲ್ಪಡುತ್ತದೆ.","code":"# Extra verified snippet for M278 L2: authorization layered separately from validation
class ToolError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, kind, message):
        self.kind = kind; self.message = message
        super().__init__(f"{kind}: {message}")

ROLE_PERMISSIONS = {
    "support_agent": {"lookup_order"},
    "admin": {"lookup_order", "issue_refund"},
}

def authorize(role, tool_name):
    allowed = ROLE_PERMISSIONS.get(role, set())
    if tool_name not in allowed:
        raise ToolError("unauthorized", f"role '{role}' may not call '{tool_name}'")
    return True

def issue_refund(order_id, amount):
    return {"order_id": order_id, "refunded": amount}

# Case 1: validation passes, but authorization genuinely rejects a support_agent calling issue_refund
try:
    authorize("support_agent", "issue_refund")
except ToolError as e:
    print(f"CASE 1 (authorization rejection despite valid arguments): kind={e.kind} message={e.message!r}")

# Case 2: same tool call, admin role, genuinely authorized and executes
authorize("admin", "issue_refund")
result = issue_refund("ORD-5521", 42.50)
print(f"CASE 2 (admin authorized, tool executes): {result}")
